markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Debugging: More Than Fixing Red Errors

Lesson 9 opens with "Find the Bug": an algorithm meant to play only a listener's favorite songs, 4 and 5-star ratings only.

<div style="border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden; margin: 12px 0 16px 0; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8;">
  <div style="background-color: #1F1976; padding: 16px 20px; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #E4E2F8; line-height: 1.8;">
for each song in list of songs:<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;look up rating for song<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;if rating &gt;= 4 or rating &lt;= 5:<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;play song<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;else:<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;skip song
  </div>
</div>

This code runs without crashing. No red error message, no exception, nothing stops it. It just plays every single song, because every rating is either 4-or-above or 5-or-below, there's no number that fails both. The `or` should be `and`.

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;">
    <strong>&#128214; Debug:</strong> to find out why your code isn't working and fix the problem so your program runs the way you expect.
  </div>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; line-height: 1.5;">
    <strong>&#128161; Teacher tip:</strong> this is the reframe to give students up front: debugging isn't just chasing error messages. A program can run perfectly and still be wrong. The skill is reading what it actually did, line by line, and comparing that to what you expected.
  </div>
</div>
MARKDOWN
